    "title": "Risks and Mitigations",
    "content": "The AI Tutor for MBA Students faces several significant risks, primarily stemming from market adoption challenges, an evolving regulatory landscape, and the current lack of validated MBA-specific demand. A key challenge is the relatively low perception of AI's benefits in education among the US public, with only 39% considering it beneficial [web-236a31a2f5a04bcb8b4ae6d39dee9e37]. This is compounded by a notable adoption gap among higher education faculty, where only 22% regularly use generative AI, compared to 83% of K-12 teachers [web-236a31a2f5a04bcb8b4ae6d39dee9e37]. These factors could significantly impede user acquisition and institutional partnerships.\n\nThe regulatory environment for AI in US education is rapidly evolving at both federal and state levels, with all 50 states having introduced AI legislation [web-57da4247bdb54dd99b32029fe644c91b]. Policies are focusing on governance, data privacy, academic integrity, and human oversight [web-ba3fef6a51f946d1a50970f9ee82838f, web-4bb0c35573164184b0269f72942c77e8]. Concerns about students using AI to cheat are high among teachers (78%) [web-22a440c045674c219e988a462c13f36c], necessitating robust features and clear guidelines to ensure ethical use and build trust.\n\nFurthermore, the precise unmet learning needs, frequency, urgency, and willingness to pay among MBA students for an AI tutor remain unconfirmed assumptions. Without this specific market validation, the product features, pricing strategy, and overall financial viability are speculative. General AI tools like ChatGPT are already widely used by students (66%) [web-236a31a2f5a04bcb8b4ae6d39dee9e37], posing a competitive threat as readily available substitutes. Mitigations include proactive engagement with regulatory bodies, developing a strong value proposition centered on academic integrity and MBA-specific pedagogy, and conducting thorough market research to validate demand and willingness to pay.",
